    "result_text": "The change is complete and the full test suite passes (`BUILD SUCCESS`).\n\n## What I did\n\nThe spec required emitting an immutable structured JSON event alongside the existing audit line, with the event carrying the owner's **current primary identifier** \u2014 the `customerCode` now, but transparently the `memberId` once that unification happens later. I made the change in three small, single-responsibility pieces:\n\n1. **`Owner.getPrimaryIdentifier()`** (new derived accessor) \u2014 returns the customer code today. This is the single seam for the future refactor: when the customer code is unified into the member id, only this method changes and the event automatically follows. Callers ask the owner for \"its identity\" rather than reaching for `customerCode` directly.\n\n2. **`OwnerCreatedEvent`** (new immutable `record`) \u2014 holds `{seq, ownerId, customerCode, membershipLevel, event}`. Records give immutability for free; a compact constructor defaults `event` to the `OWNER_CREATED` discriminator so callers can't set it wrong. It's a pure data holder with no logic.\n\n3. **`OwnerAuditLogger.logCreated(...)`** \u2014 extended to build the event and serialize it to JSON on the same `AUDIT` trail after the existing line. It owns the monotonic sequence via an `AtomicLong` (thread-safe across concurrent creates) and sources the identifier from `owner.getPrimaryIdentifier()`. Audit side-effects stayed entirely inside this existing audit component \u2014 no new logic leaked into the controller or service.\n\nThe emitted event:\n```\n{\"seq\":1,\"ownerId\":11,\"customerCode\":\"NSW-16E6609E\",\"membershipLevel\":1,\"event\":\"OWNER_CREATED\"}\n```\n\nI reused the existing `tools.jackson` (Jackson 3) `ObjectMapper` already on the classpath and the existing derived `getMembershipLevel()` accessor rather than recomputing anything. The new `AcceptanceTest` and all existing tests pass.",
